ode. Hmm. Let me reconsider: the current code has `liveNeighborCount`. If I switch survival to use neighborCounts, `liveNeighborCount` becomes unused. Leaving unused code is bad. Removing it is part of the green change (the new implementation supersedes it). I think it's fine to remove it as part of the green rewrite since the implementation is being extended. The green skill allows rewriting the body; \"no refactoring yet\" means don't improve structure gratuitously, but replacing the counting mechanism to support births is necessary.\n\nActually, let me keep it simpler and closer to the existing structure to minimize churn: keep `liveNeighborCount` for survival (it already works), and ADD the neighborCounts map ONLY for births. This is the smallest diff:\n\n```ts\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const liveCells = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => cellKey(x, y)));\n  const liveNeighborCount = ([x, y]: Cell): number =>\n    NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS.filter(([dx, dy]) => liveCells.has(cellKey(x + dx, y + dy))).length;\n  const survivors = cells.filter((cell) => liveNeighborCount(cell) >= MIN_NEIGHBORS_TO_SURVIVE);\n\n  const neighborCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      const k = cellKey(x + dx, y + dy);\n      neighborCounts.set(k, (neighborCounts.get(k) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n  const births: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const [k, count] of neighborCounts) {\n    if (count === 3 && !liveCells.has(k)) {\n      const [x, y] = k.split(\",\").map(Number);\n      births.push([x, y]);\n    }\n  }\n  return [...survivors, ...births];\n}\n```\n\nThis keeps `liveNeighborCount` (used for survivors) and adds the map for births. There's duplication (two ways to count neighbors) but that's a refactor opportunity for the subagent. For green, this is the minimal addition. Good — it leaves refactor work for the subagent (consolidating the two counting mechanisms), which is exactly what TDD