y) ?? 0` correctly returns 0 for such a cell. Good — the single-cell test: cell (0,0) has 0 live neighbors, count = 0, 0 >= 2 is false, so it doesn't survive. Correct.\n\nTwo adjacent cells (0,1),(1,1): each has 1 live neighbor. (0,1) is a neighbor of (1,1)? (0,1) is at offset (-1,0) from (1,1) — yes, neighbor. So neighborCounts will have (0,1)→1 and (1,1)→1. 1 >= 2 false → both die. Correct.\n\nBlock: each cell has 3 live neighbors → count 3 → 3 >= 2 survive. And births? The dead cells adjacent to the block: cells with 3 live neighbors would be born. For a 2x2 block at (0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(1,1), the dead cells with 3 live neighbors... let me think. A dead cell at (-1,0) has neighbors (-2,-1),(-1,-1),(0,-1),(-2,0),(0,0),(-2,1),(-1,1),(0,1). Live among these: (0,0) and (0,1) → 2 neighbors. Not 3. Dead cell at (0,-1): neighbors (-1,-2),(0,-2),(1,-2),(-1,-1),(1,-1),(-1,0),(1,0)... wait (0,-1) neighbors: (-1,-2),(0,-2),(1,-2),(-1,-1),(1,-1),(-1,0),(1,0)... that's only listing 7, the 8th is... neighbors of (0,-1): (-1,-2),(0,-2),(1,-2),(-1,-1),(1,-1),(-1,0),(1,0),(0,0)? No wait. (0,-1) + offsets:\n(-1,-2)? No. Offset (-1,-1) → (-1,-2). Offset (0,-1) → (0,-2). Offset (1,-1) → (1,-2). Offset (-1,0) → (-1,-1). Offset (1,0) → (1,-1). Offset (-1,1) → (-1,0). Offset (0,1) → (0,0). Offset (1,1) → (1,0).\nLive among these: (0,0) and (1,0) → 2. Not 3.\nSo no births for the block. The block is a still life. Correct — block unchanged.\n\nL-tromino (0,0),(1,0),(0,1): \n- Survivors: each live cell has 2 live neighbors → 2 >= 2 → all 3 survive.\n- Births: dead cells with exactly 3 live neighbors. (1,1): neighbors (0,0),(1,0),(0,1) and others. Live: (0,0) [offset (-1,-1)], (1,0) [offset (0,-1)], (0,1) [offset (-1,0)]. That's 3 → born. Any other dead cell with 3? Let me check (0,0)'s... actually let me check all candidates. The dead cells that are neighbors of at least one live cell and have count 3. (1,1) has 3. Others? Let me see neighborCounts entries with value 3. \n  - (1,1): neighbors are the 3 live cells → 3. Born.\n  - Are there others? A dead cell needs 3 of the L's cells as neighbors. The L is (0,0),(1,0),(0,1). Which dead cells are adjacent to all... not all, but 3 of them. (1,1) is adjacent to all 3. Any other dead cell adjacent to 3? (2,0)? neighbors of (2,0): (1,-1),(2,-1),(3,-1),(1,0),(3,0),(1,1),(2,1),(3,1). Live: (1,0). Just 1. No.\n  - So only (1,1) born. Total: 3 survivors + 1 birth = 4 cells = block. Correct!\n\nGreat, the refactoring preserves all behaviors.\n\nNow let me reconsider — is there an even cleaner unification? I could iterate over `neighborCounts` once for both survivors and births:\n\n```typescript\nconst next: Cell[] = [];\nfor (const [key, count] of neighborCounts) {\n  const isAlive = liveCells.has(key);\n  if ((isAlive && count >= MIN_NEIGHBORS_TO_SURVIVE) || (!isAlive && count === EXACT_NEIGHBORS_TO_BIRTH)) {\n    const [x, y] = key.split(\",\").map(Number);\n    next.push([x, y]);\n  }\n}\n// But this misses live cells with 0 neighbors that should die (they're not in neighborCounts, so correctly omitted) \n// AND live cells with 0 neighbors are correctly not in next.\n```\n\nWait, but this approach also correctly handles live cells with 0 neighbors (they're not in neighborCounts, so they're not added → they die). \n\nBut hold on — the birth rule `count === 3` uses a literal 3. The task says \"The birth rule uses literal `3`.\" Should I extract it to a named constant? That would improve intent (Rule 2). Let me consider: `EXACT_NEIGHBORS_FOR_BIRTH = 3`. That reveals intent better than a bare `3`. But it adds an element (Rule 4 tradeoff). Rule 2 (reveals intent) trumps Rule 4 (fewest elements), so extracting the constant is good.\n\nBut wait — the survival threshold is `MIN_NEIGHBORS_TO_SURVIVE = 2` with `>=`. There's no upper bound yet (overpopulation not tested). So I should NOT add an upper bound (that would be implementing ahead — violates TDD). The current `>= 2` is fine for the 5 passing tests. I'll keep it as is.\n\nHmm, but should I touch the birth constant? The task explicitly notes \"The birth rule uses literal `3`.\" This is a hint that extracting it would improve intent. But it's also a slight Rule 4 cost. Given Rule 2 > Rule 4, and the literal `3` is a magic number, extracting `EXACT_NEIGHBORS_FOR_BIRTH = 3` improves clarity. Let me do that.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the single-loop unification. It reduces duplication further (one loop instead of filter + separate loop) and reduces mass. But does it reveal intent as clearly? The two-step version (survivors filter + births loop) is arguably more readable — it separates the two rules of the Game of Life (survival and birth). The single-loop version combines them, which is slightly less intent-revealing but eliminates the double iteration.\n\nHmm. Rule 2 (reveals intent) trumps Rule 3 (DRY) and Rule 4. Let me think about which is more intent-revealing.\n\nThe two-mechanism problem the task highlights is the duplication between `liveNeighborCount` (filter) and `neighborCounts` (Map). The cleanest fix that removes duplication while keeping intent clear:\n\nOption A: Keep `neighborCounts` Map, use it for both survival (via filter on cells) and births (via loop). Two separate passes but shared data structure.\n\nOption B: Single loop over `neighborCounts` deciding survival/birth per cell.\n\nOption A keeps the two rules visually separated (survivors line, births loop), which is more intent-revealing. Option B is more DRY but merges the rules.\n\nI'll go with Option A — it removes the conceptual duplication (two neighbor-counting mechanisms → one) while keeping the two Life rules clearly separated. This best satisfies Rule 2 and Rule 3 together.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. In Option A, the survivors filter does `neighborCounts.get(cellKey(x, y)) ?? 0`. This re-derives the key and looks it up. That's fine. But there's a subtle duplication: we already have `liveCells` (Set of keys) and we iterate `cells` (array). For survivors we iterate `cells` and look up each in the Map. That's clean.\n\nLet me also reconsider whether I even need `liveCells` Set. It's used for:\n1. (Before) `liveNeighborCount` — removed.\n2. (After) survivors — no longer needed (we use the Map).\n3. Births: `!liveCells.has(key)` to skip already-live cells.\n\nSo `liveCells` is still needed for the births check (to avoid birthing a cell that's already alive — though a live cell with exactly 3 neighbors should survive anyway, so birthing it would be a duplicate; the `!liveCells.has(key)` prevents double-counting). Actually, if a live cell has exactly 3 neighbors, it survives (via survivors filter) AND would be \"born\" (count===3) without the check → duplicate in output. So the check is needed to avoid duplicates. Keep `liveCells`.\n\nActually wait — could I avoid `liveCells` by checking survivors separately? In Option B (single loop), I check `isAlive = liveCells.has(key)`. Still need it. In Option A, the births loop needs `!liveCells.has(key)`. So `liveCells` stays. Good.\n\nNow, mass calculation for AFTER (Option A):\n\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const liveCells = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => cellKey(x, y)));\n\n  const neighborCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      const key = cellKey(x + dx, y + dy);\n      neighborCounts.set(key, (neighborCounts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const survivors = cells.filter(([x, y]) =>\n    (neighborCounts.get(cellKey(x, y)) ?? 0) >= MIN_NEIGHBORS_TO_SURVIVE\n  );\n\n  const births: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const [key, count] of neighborCounts) {\n    if (count === EXACT_NEIGHBORS_FOR_BIRTH && !liveCells.has(key)) {\n      const [x, y] = key.split(\",\").map(Number);\n      births.push([x, y]);\n    }\n  }\n  return [...survivors, ...births];\n}\n```\n\nPlus module-level add: `const EXACT_NEIGHBORS_FOR_BIRTH = 3;` → binding 1, assignment 6, constant 3 → 8. But we remove the literal `3` from the if (was constant 1). Net module change: +8 -1 = +7? Wait, but the birth rule previously had `count === 3` with literal 3 (constant 1). Now it's `count === EXACT_NEIGHBORS_FOR_BIRTH` (binding 1). So in the if: -1 constant, +1 binding = net 0 there, plus new module constant line +8.\n\nHmm, that's a mass INCREASE from extracting the constant. But Rule 2 (reveals intent) trumps APP mass. The named constant is clearer. I'll note this tradeoff explicitly.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. Is extracting `EXACT_NEIGHBORS_FOR_BIRTH` worth it? The literal `3` next to `count === 3` in a births context is fairly clear (\"3 neighbors to be born\"). But `MIN_NEIGHBORS_TO_SURVIVE = 2` already sets a precedent for naming survival threshold. For symmetry and intent, naming the birth threshold is consistent. I think it's a reasonable Rule 2 improvement. But it does add mass.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. The PRIMARY refactoring the task points at is removing the duplication between the two neighbor-counting mechanisms. That's the big win. Let me make sure that's the focus and calculate its mass impact, then optionally do the constant extraction as a secondary Rule 2 improvement.\n\nThe big win: removing `liveNeighborCount` (the filter-based arrow function + its usage). Let me compute the delta.\n\nBEFORE had (items 6 and 7):\n- Item 6 (liveNeighborCount definition): 22\n- Item 7 (survivors with liveNeighborCount call): 22\nTotal for survival mechanism: 44\n\nAFTER survival mechanism:\n- survivors filter using neighborCounts.get:\n  - binding survivors: 1, assignment 6\n  - cells.filter: invocation 2, loop 5\n  - params x, y: 2\n  - neighborCounts.get: invocation 2\n  - cellKey: invocation 2\n  - ?? : conditional 4\n  - 0 constant: 1\n  - >= : conditional 4\n  - MIN_NEIGHBORS_TO_SURVIVE: binding 1\n  - Subtotal: 1+6+2+5+2+2+2+4+1+4+1 = 30\n\nSo survival mechanism: 44 → 30, saving 14.\n\nBut we also need to make sure `neighborCounts` is built BEFORE survivors now (reordering). In BEFORE, neighborCounts was built after survivors. In AFTER, neighborCounts must be built before survivors (since survivors uses it). This is just reordering, no mass change to those blocks.\n\nSo the net mass change from the unification: -14 (survival mechanism), plus the constant extraction +7 if I do it.\n\nLet me reconsider whether to extract the constant. Given the task says \"The birth rule uses literal `3`\" as an observation, and the focus is on the duplication, I think the cleanest single refactoring is the unification. I could mention the constant as a possible future improvement but the overpopulation upper bound isn't tested yet, so the survival rule is `>= 2` (open-ended) which is a bit asymmetric with a birth rule of `=== 3`. \n\nHmm, actually, let me think about whether extracting the birth constant is the right call now. Since overpopulation isn't tested, the survival rule will change soon (next test adds the upper bound `> 3 dies`, i.e., survive if 2 or 3). If I extract `EXACT_NEIGHBORS_FOR_BIRTH = 3` now, when the survival rule becomes \"2 or 3\", there might be a shared `3`. But that's future-coupling; TDD says don't build for untested behavior. I'll keep it simple: do the unification (removes real duplication), and extract the birth constant for intent clarity since it parallels the existing `MIN_NEIGHBORS_TO_SURVIVE`. \n\nActually, you know what, let me reconsider. The literal `3` appears once. `MIN_NEIGHBORS_TO_SURVIVE = 2` appears once. Extracting one magic number that appears once is a minor Rule 2 gain but a Rule 4 cost (new element). Given Rule 2 > Rule 4, and the parallelism aids readability, I'll extract it. It's defensible.\n\nHmm, but actually, I want to be careful not to over-engineer. Let me focus on the clearest, highest-value refactoring: eliminating the duplicate neighbor-counting mechanism. That's unambiguously good (Rule 3, and the resulting code is also clearer — Rule 2, because there's now one obvious source of truth for \"how many live neighbors does each cell have\").\n\nFor the birth constant: I'll extract it too, because it makes the two rules symmetric and self-documenting, and it parallels the existing survival constant. This is a Rule 2 improvement that's worth the small mass cost. I'll document the tradeoff.\n\nLet me reconsider — actually, maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me do BOTH as one coherent refactoring pass: (1) unify neighbor counting (removes duplication, the headline change), (2) name the birth threshold (intent). Both are Simple Design improvements. I'll calculate mass for the whole thing.\n\nLet me reconsider the survivors line once more for clarity:\n\n```typescript\nconst survivors = cells.filter(([x, y]) =>\n  (neighborCounts.get(cellKey(x, y)) ?? 0) >= MIN_NEIGHBORS_TO_SURVIVE\n);\n```\n\nThis reads: \"survivors are live cells whose live-neighbor count is at least the survival minimum.\" Clear. The `?? 0` handles live cells with zero neighbors (not in the map). Good.\n\nAlternatively I could write a tiny helper `liveNeighborCountOf = (key) => neighborCounts.get(key) ?? 0`. But that's adding an element (Rule 4) for marginal clarity. The inline `?? 0` is clear enough. Skip the helper.\n\nNow, one more consideration: the survivors filter iterates `cells` (the input array), which may contain duplicates? The tests don't have duplicate cells. The `liveCells` Set dedupes for the births check. For survivors, if `cells` had duplicates, we'd get duplicate survivors. But that's pre-existing behavior (the old `liveNeighborCount`-based filter also iterated `cells` with potential dupes). Not my concern for this refactoring — keep behavior identical. Good.\n\nLet me also double check: in BEFORE, `liveNeighborCount` used `liveCells` Set (deduped) to count neighbors. In AFTER, `neighborCounts` is built from `cells` (raw, possibly with dupes). If `cells` has a duplicate live cell, the Map would double-count it as a neighbor. But again, tests have no dupes, and the old code's `neighborCounts` was ALSO built from raw `cells` (for births). So birth behavior re: dupes is unchanged. Only survival changed mechanism — and for non-duped input (all tests), `neighborCounts.get(cellKey(x,y))` for a live cell equals the number of its live neighbors, same as old `liveNeighborCount`. Identical behavior. \n\nLet me now write the refactored file and run tests.\n\nLet me also reconsider: should I keep `liveNeighborCount` name discussion? The task says \"Naming is first priority.\" Let me do the naming evaluation:\n\n- `nextGeneration` — reveals intent. Keep.\n- `cellKey` — reveals intent (converts cell to key). Keep.\n- `NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS` — reveals intent. Keep.\n- `MIN_NEIGHBORS_TO_SURVIVE` — reveals intent. Keep.\n- `liveNeighborCount` — being removed (it was the duplicated mechanism). N/A after refactor.\n- `neighborCounts` — reveals intent (Map of neighbor counts). Keep.\n- `liveCells`, `survivors`, `births` — all reveal intent. Keep.\n\nNew: `EXACT_NEIGHBORS_FOR_BIRTH` — reveals intent (the exact count for birth). Good name.\n\nNo renames needed for existing well-named identifiers. The removed `liveNeighborCount` resolves the naming/structural overlap.\n\nNow let me write it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the constant name. `MIN_NEIGHBORS_TO_SURVIVE` uses \"MIN\". For birth it's an exact value, so `EXACT_NEIGHBORS_FOR_BIRTH` or `NEIGHBORS_FOR_BIRTH` or `BIRTH_NEIGHBOR_COUNT`. I'll use `EXACT_NEIGHBORS_FOR_BIRTH` to emphasize it's an exact match (===), paralleling the survival \"MIN\" (>=). Good.\n\nLet me write the refactored implementation.\n\nLet me now compute the AFTER mass more carefully for the whole file, then delta.\n\nAFTER file:\n\nModule level:\n1. NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS: 23 (unchanged)\n2. MIN_NEIGHBORS_TO_SURVIVE = 2: 8 (unchanged)\n3. EXACT_NEIGHBORS_FOR_BIRTH = 3: binding 1, assignment 6, constant 3 → 8 (NEW)\n4. cellKey: 10 (unchanged)\n\nFunction body:\n5. function de