at mass 13. Net: -8 + 13 = +5. So mass INCREASES by ~5.\n\nHmm. So by APP mass, this refactoring is slightly WORSE (higher mass). The trade-off is: slightly higher mass, but better Rule 2 (intent) and Rule 3 (DRY).\n\nPer the guidelines: \"Rule 2 trumps APP: Clarity over low mass.\" So if this meaningfully improves clarity/DRY, it's justified despite the mass increase.\n\nBut is it a meaningful enough clarity win to justify +5 mass? The inline parse `key.split(\",\").map(Number)` is reasonably readable. Encapsulating it names the intent (\"cell from key\") and centralizes the format. It's a defensible but marginal call.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Maybe there's a refactoring that REDUCES mass while keeping or improving clarity.\n\nLet me reconsider the whole function for a mass-reducing opportunity.\n\nIdea: The `survivors` filter and `births` loop both iterate and check counts. Could I unify into a single pass over `neighborCounts`? Let me think.\n\nActually, here's a cleaner unified approach:\n\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const liveCells = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => cellKey(x, y)));\n\n  const neighborCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      const key = cellKey(x + dx, y + dy);\n      neighborCounts.set(key, (neighborCounts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const next: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const [key, count] of neighborCounts) {\n    const isAlive = liveCells.has(key);\n    const survives = isAlive && count >= MIN_NEIGHBORS_TO_SURVIVE && count <= MAX_NEIGHBORS_TO_SURVIVE;\n    const isBorn = !isAlive && count === EXACT_NEIGHBORS_FOR_BIRTH;\n    if (survives || isBorn) {\n      const [x, y] = key.split(\",\").map(Number);\n      next.push([x, y]);\n    }\n  }\n  return next;\n}\n```\n\nWait, but this changes behavior! The current code computes survivors by iterating `cells` (the original live cells), not `neighborCounts`. A live ce